study.recommendation:
For the 2027-01-11 quarterly review (information only; nothing changes before then): the long-sample data give no reliable evidence for a semiconductor-peer earnings-night blackout. Peer nights did not have lower mean NVDA returns, higher variance or fatter tails over 2017-2026. A blackout would have lowered geometric growth by an estimated -2.6bp/night (CI -7.5 to +2.4), would not have lowered max drawdown, and would not have changed the per-night chance of a -5% 2x night.

The only adverse signals are weak and should not drive a rule:
- the last 2 years (t=-0.6);
- am-only nights (t=-0.6);
- the rest-5 tail rate (Fisher p=0.005, about 27 tests run);
- MRVL/ASML per-peer means (t about -1.4).
They can be re-checked at the review with the shadow logs.

The two live losses (TSM 10/15, LRCX 10/22) are consistent with ordinary NVDA overnight volatility. About 4.7% of all nights are at or below -2.5% 1x, roughly 1 in 21.

Under Variant A's peer set, the upcoming candidate nights are:
- 10/27->10/28: not an event night (SK hynix is not covered);
- 10/28->10/29: KLAC, 36 nights, mean +38.7bp;
- 10/29->10/30: INTC, mean -5.3bp, t=-0.5;
- 11/03->11/04: AMD and SMCI;
- 11/04->11/05: ARM and QCOM;
- 11/12->11/13: AMAT.
None justifies acting differently.

A more consequential issue for the review: at the assumed 0.3% round-trip cost per night, the 2x NVDA overnight hold itself had negative long-run log growth. Measured execution cost and the edge-decay switch matter far more than a peer blackout.
